\begin_body

\begin_layout Section

\lang english
Can rising consumption deepen inequality?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang english
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:introduction"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
 Economic systems can be viewed as complex systems whose macroscopic properties emerge from interactions among many heterogeneous agents
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "JUSUP20221,yakovenko2009,Farmer2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This perspective,
 developed in econophysics,
 has provided important insights into empirical regularities,
 particularly in the distribution of income and wealth
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "shaikh"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In fact,
 in econophysics,
 a central problem is to identify the mechanisms responsible for the emergence of highly unequal income and wealth distributions,
 typically characterized by broad,
 often heavy-tailed forms observed across different economies and time periods.
 While only a limited number of modeling approaches can reproduce these distributions,
 often capturing only specific features,
 identifying the underlying mechanisms responsible for their robustness and universality remains a major challenge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Motivated by this approach,
 a variety of models have been proposed to describe the dynamics of wealth and income,
 including kinetic exchange models and agent-based frameworks.
 Within this class of models,
 the Social Architecture of Capitalism (SA) model
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wright_2005"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 proposed by Ian Wright,
 offers a simple yet realistic representation of economic interactions between agents,reproducing key empirical features of wealth distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In a recent work
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Borba2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we revisited the SA model and systematically explored a wide range of its parameters.
 A two-class structure of wealth distribution emerges,
 producing an upper tail following a Pareto power-law
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dragulescu2001,yakovenko2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and a lower segment characterized by an exponential or lognormal form.
 We identified a key parameter that is responsible for opening a gap between the two groups.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
This key parameter,
 denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 is defined as the ratio between the average wealth per capita and the mean salary.
 We showed that the resulting wealth distribution,
 its characteristic patterns,
 the eventual separation of wealth,
 and the extinction of the middle class for large values of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 depend primarily on this parameter,
 with only a weak dependence on system size.
 In particular,
 inequality as summarized by the Gini index
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "PLATAPEREZ201579"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 was found to increase monotonically with 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 This prediction of the model is consistent with results obtained from empirical data in which 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}$
\end_inset

 are estimated using average employee compensation and net personal wealth,
 respectively,
 based,
 among other sources,
 on data from the World Inequality Database (WID) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "WID"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
However,
 the model relies on strong simplifying assumptions that may oversimplify the complex dynamics of real economies.
 For instance,
 in the model all transactions (salary payments,
 purchases,
 and revenue collections) occur with the same frequency,
 i.e.,
 at every time step.
 In real economies,
 however,
 purchases typically occur more frequently than the reception of salaries or the collection of revenues.
 We therefore aim to investigate how relaxing this rule,
 by introducing different frequencies for these processes relative to salary periodicity,
 may affect the results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
A similar question arises regarding the range of possible expenses (or revenue collections),
 which in the original formulation can take any value between zero and the agent’s total wealth (or total market value).
 In other words,
 agents are allowed to spend their entire wealth (or collect the entire market value) in a single step.
 Here,
 we explore how limiting these maxima—
both expenditures and market value collections—
affects the model’s dynamics and the resulting inequality patterns.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Furthermore,
 we sought to investigate how the system behaves in alternative formulations in which the average wage is no longer fixed exogenously but instead emerges endogenously from the internal model dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
The model is defined in Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 with emphasis on the introduced variations.
 Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:results"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 presents the results of the agent-based simulations,
 examining how consumption rules,
 market revenues,
 and employment regulations affect inequality.
 Finally,
 Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:final"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 provides the conclusions and final remarks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang english
Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:model"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
The agent-based model implemented in this paper introduces variations in the original SA model proposed by Ian Wright
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wright_2005"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
  This model is based on the assumption that `social relations of production' 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "marx2013capital1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 define the roles that individuals play in economic activity.
 In particular,
 the employer–employee relationship mediated by wage labor is taken as an intrinsic feature of capitalism.
 In this context,
 the structure of inequality emerges from the organization of these relationships,
 insofar as a small class of capitalists receives its income in the form of profits,
 while a large class of workers has wages as its sole source of income.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
This approach allows one to abstract from specific and often transitory characteristics of concrete capitalist economies,
 such as natural or technical constraints or particular types of commodities,
 and to focus on interactions between agents mediated by monetary exchange.
 The model thus reduces the economy to a minimal structure in which individuals,
 differentiated by their position in the relations of production,
 interact through money flows,
 and this dynamics is responsible for generating the patterns of inequality observed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
The society consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 agents (individuals or other economic entities,
 identified by the label 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\dots,N$
\end_inset

).
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has a quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}(t)$
\end_inset

 representing wealth,
 which varies over time.
 Although in the original proposal money takes integer values as multiples of a monetary unit,
 we have verified that no significant differences are observed when considering real values,
 which is the choice in the present work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
The size of the population 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 and the total wealth of the system are conserved,
 so the wealth per capita 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}=W/N$
\end_inset

 is constant.
 At any given time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 agents belong to one of three classes:
 employees (working class),
 employers (capitalist class),
 or unemployed.
 The class to which each agent belongs is dynamically updated.
 Then each agent is characterized by an index 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 that identifies their employer:
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=j$
\end_inset

 indicates that agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is the employer of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 There are two special cases:
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 indicates that the agent is unemployed and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=-1$
\end_inset

 indicates that they are an employer.
 Thus,
 at any time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 the state of the entire economy is defined by the set of pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $S(t)=\left\{ \left(w_{i}(t),e_{i}(t)\right):1\leq i\leq N\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 It is important to note that a firm consists of a set of employees and a single employer who is the sole owner of the firm,
 and there is no self-employment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In addition to agents,
 the model includes a market entity that represents a monetary pool with value 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 and evolves through transactions.
 Consumer expenditures increase 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

,
 while firm revenues decrease it,
 so firms compete for shares of this pool rather than being paid directly by individual buyers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In its original formulation,
 the model is specified by four parameters:
 the number of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $N,$
\end_inset

 the bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $(p_{a},p_{b})$
\end_inset

 of the wage distribution,
 and the total money supply 
\begin_inset Formula $W,$
\end_inset

 which fixes the initial average wealth per capita 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}=W/N$
\end_inset

.
 In practice,
 however,
 we have shown that the macroscopic behavior of the system does not depend on the individual values of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{b}$
\end_inset

,
 but only on their mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}=(p_{a}+p_{b})/2$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Borba2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 We therefore fix a constant unit wage,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}=p$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 sets the monetary unit,
 the only remaining intensive control parameter is the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $R=\overline{w}/\overline{p}$
\end_inset

,
 which measures the wealth per capita in units of the average wage.
 The model thus depends effectively on 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 with a weak residual dependence on the system size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 In practice,
 no significant finite-size effects are observed for 
\begin_inset Formula $N\gtrsim20$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In what follows,
 we summarize the original rules,
 emphasizing the modifications.
 In the initial state,
 all agents have the same wealth (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}(0)=\overline{w}$
\end_inset

) and the same state (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}(0)=0$
\end_inset

),
 and the following set of five rules is applied,
 in the given order,
 after randomly selecting an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Hiring
\series default
 – If 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is unemployed (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=0$
\end_inset

),
 a potential employer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected from the set 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 of all agents who are not employees (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}\leq0$
\end_inset

),
 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(j)=w_{j}/\sum_{n\in H}w_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\ge p$
\end_inset

,
 agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is hired by 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 (thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=j$
\end_inset

).
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 was previously unemployed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 becomes an employer (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}=-1$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Expenditure
\series default
 (on goods and services) – A random agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected,
 and an amount 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

,
 representing the agent's expenditure,
 is subtracted from their wealth (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\to w_{j}-w$
\end_inset

) and added to the market value (
\begin_inset Formula $V\to V+w$
\end_inset

).
 We modify this rule,
 introducing two new parameters,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{E}$
\end_inset

 associated with the frequency of visits to the market,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{E}$
\end_inset

 representing the fraction of wealth spent in the market.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{E}\leq1$
\end_inset

,
 it denotes the probability that this rule will be applied.
 Otherwise,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{E}$
\end_inset

 indicates the number of times the rule is applied.
 For each application,
 a random agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected and a random expenditure value 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is drawn from the range 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,\Phi_{E}w_{j}\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Market revenue
\series default
 (from sales of goods and services) – A random agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected,
 and an amount 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is transferred from the market value (
\begin_inset Formula $V\to V-w$
\end_inset

) to the agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is an employer,
 the amount is credited to 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\to w_{j}+w$
\end_inset

),
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is an employee,
 it is credited to the employer 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{e_{j}}\to w_{e_{j}}+w$
\end_inset

).
 In all cases,
 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 represents revenue for the owner of the company.
 We modify this rule,
 introducing two new parameters,
 namely,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}$
\end_inset

,
 associated with the frequency of visits to the market,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}$
\end_inset

,
 representing the fraction of wealth withdrawn from the market value.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}\leq1$
\end_inset

,
 it denotes the probability that this rule will be applied.
 Otherwise,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}$
\end_inset

 specifies the number of times the rule is applied.
 For each application,
 a new random agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected;
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is not unemployed (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\neq0$
\end_inset

),
 a random value is drawn from the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in[0,\Phi_{M}V]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Firing –
\series default
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 acts as an employer (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=-1$
\end_inset

),
 its financial constraint determines how many employees must be dismissed.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the number of agents employed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 those with 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}=i$
\end_inset

).
 Since each worker is paid a wage 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

,
 the employer can afford at most 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}/p$
\end_inset

 employees.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}>w_{i}/p$
\end_inset

,
 the excess number of workers 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n=\max\!\left(n_{i}-\frac{w_{i}}{p},\,0\right)
\]

\end_inset

is dismissed.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 agents to be fired are selected uniformly at random from 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

’s list of employees.
 If all employees are dismissed,
 agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 becomes unemployed (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=0$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Wage payment –
\series default
 For each employee 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 of an employer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}=i$
\end_inset

),
 the wage 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is paid by 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 resulting in the updates 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\to w_{i}-p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\to w_{j}+p$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
The update is asynchronous,
 meaning that the state of the system is updated after each individual rule is applied.
 A sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 rule applications (one Monte Carlo step) corresponds,
 arbitrarily,
 to one month,
 giving each agent,
 on average,
 the opportunity to be selected once per month.
 We verified that no significant differences arise when changing the order of the rules;
 therefore,
 they are applied in random order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
This set of rules,
 including the modifications introduced in the 
\shape italic
Expenditure
\shape default
 and 
\shape italic
Market revenue
\shape default
 rules that govern market exchanges,
 is used to obtain the results presented in Sections
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:expenditure"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:revenue"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 The code is publicly available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Below,
 we consider an alternative variant of the model obtained by modifying the rules related to employment.
 This version is used exclusively for the results presented in Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:hiring"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*

\lang english
Modifications in employment rules
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "modifications"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
We propose a modification of the 
\shape italic
Hiring
\shape default
 rule in which wages are no longer fixed but instead emerge from the system dynamics.
 Furthermore,
 agents are allowed to seek alternative employment offering higher wages even while already employed,
 a realistic feature absent in the original model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
 By removing the fixed-wage constraint,
 the model captures a more dynamic labor market with varying wage levels.
 The rationale for this modification is as follows:
 unemployed workers seek employment,
 and the longer they remain unemployed,
 the more they lower their wage expectations,
 reflecting a weakened bargaining position.
 Conversely,
 employed agents search for alternative opportunities offering higher wages than their current ones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Under this rule,
 in contrast to the fixed-wage model,
 both wages and expectations adjust endogenously to labor market conditions through a feedback mechanism.
 This coupling between labor market conditions and wage formation drives the evolution of income and wealth distributions over time.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
To enable this feature,
 we introduce two agent-specific variables and one system parameter.
 The variables are 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 which records the last wage received by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 which represents the wage expected agent by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from their employer.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 controls the pressure governing wage adjustments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In Ref.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wright2008implicit"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Wright analyzed the emergence of stylized economic facts for 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

.
 Here,
 we extend this analysis by examining how the system’s behavior changes as 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is varied.
 These extensions require a reformulation of the 
\shape italic
Firing
\shape default
 and 
\shape italic
Wage payment
\shape default
 rules,
 which are directly related to wage dynamics.
 The modified rules are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Hiring
\series default
 – If 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is not an employer (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\geq0$
\end_inset

):
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang english
A potential employer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected from the set 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 of all agents except employees (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}\neq0$
\end_inset

),
 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(j)=w_{j}/\sum_{n\in H}w_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang english
A wage offer 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is drawn uniformly from the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\eta_{i},\left(1+\alpha\right)\eta_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the agent's wage expectation and the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 therefore controls the intensity of the wage increase sought by the agent.
 This choice is inspired by Isaac’s analysis of the CSA model,
 in which he proposes replacing Wright’s original interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\eta_{i},2\eta_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

 with a fixed incremental adjustment of 1%,
 aiming to mitigate the excessively high unemployment rates observed in the original model
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ISAAC2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 exceeds the wealth of the selected employer (
\begin_inset Formula $w>w_{j}$
\end_inset

),
 it is truncated to 
\begin_inset Formula $w=w_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang english
If the final wage offer exceeds the agent’s expectation (
\begin_inset Formula $w>\eta_{i}$
\end_inset

),
 agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is hired by employer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=j$
\end_inset

),
 and the expectation is updated to 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{i}=w$
\end_inset

.
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is not hired and was originally unemployed,
 a new expectation is drawn uniformly from the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,\eta_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\lang english
Wage payment and firing
\series default
 – If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is an employer (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}<0$
\end_inset

),
 we define the set 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 of employees of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 as those agents 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}=i$
\end_inset

.
 For each employee 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in E$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang english
If employer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has sufficient funds,
 an amount equal to the employee’s expectation 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is transferred from employer (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\rightarrow w_{i}-\eta_{j}$
\end_inset

) to employee (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\rightarrow w_{j}+\eta_{j}$
\end_inset

),
 and the employee’s last received wage is updated to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{j}=\eta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang english
If employer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 does not have sufficient funds,
 employee 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is fired,
 and their wage expectation is reset to the last received wage,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{j}=\mu_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 if the agent is fired before receiving the negotiated wage,
 the expectation is not realized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang english
If,
 after processing the entire set 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

,
 all employees have been fired,
 employer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 becomes unemployed (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=0$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
