\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Unlike the changes in the 
\shape italic
Expenditure
\shape default
 rule,
 now the GDP remains unchanged (not shown).
 That is,
 modifications in how money is withdrawn from the market value do not affect consumption,
 therefore keeping GDP constant.
 Since,
 in this model,
 GDP is given by the aggregate annual income of capitalists,
 this also implies that the total income appropriated by capitalists does not change.
 The labor share likewise remains constant,
 meaning that the fraction of total annual income allocated to workers’ wages is unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Although these indices remain constant,
 a subtle but systematic variation in the Gini index can be observed across all figures corresponding to modifications of the 
\shape italic
Market Revenue
\shape default
 rule.
 Specifically,
 inequality increases with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}$
\end_inset

 and decreases with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}$
\end_inset

.
 This pattern indicates that the observed change in inequality must originate from variations within classes rather than between them.
 Since wages are fixed in the model and the total income allocated to workers remains unchanged,
 the variation in inequality must therefore arise within the capitalist class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
This effect is reflected in the behavior of the HHI,
 which closely tracks the variation of the Gini coefficient.
 Increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}$
\end_inset

 raises the fraction of market value appropriated by firms at each step;
 as a result,
 there is a slight increase in monopoly power and,
 consequently,
 in the Gini coefficient.
 This mechanism becomes particularly pronounced when there has been a large deposit that makes the market value 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 exceed its average level.
 In this case,
 increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}$
\end_inset

 implies that a larger fraction of the accumulated value is withdrawn each time the 
\shape italic
Market revenue
\shape default
 rule is applied,
 and less of this value will be distributed among capitalists,
 increasing the concentration of GDP distribution and,
 consequently,
 the concentration of wealth.
 In extreme cases with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}=1$
\end_inset

,
 the entire market value could be appropriated by a single capitalist.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
It is worth noting that,
 regardless of the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}$
\end_inset

,
 GDP does not increase.
 This means that it does not change consumption in the system or the total money available in market value to be appropriated by capitalists throughout the year.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Now,
 when we increase 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}$
\end_inset

,
 we observe a more pronounced effect on the variation of inequality for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}\leq1$
\end_inset

.
 In this case,
 the lower the frequency,
 the more often agents can deposit money in the market through consumption before the agent withdraws it,
 increasing the total amount of money available for withdraw.
 So now,
 the effect of varying the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}$
\end_inset

 frequency no longer depends as much on above-average deposits,
 its influence is more strongly felt in the aggregate of ordinary transactions,
 especially depending on the amount of market value withdrawals that exist between one deposit and another.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Remember that we now maintain 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}=1$
\end_inset

,
 which means the worker can withdraw all the money available in the market at once.
 Thus,
 we can hypothesize that increasing the frequency has the effect of preventing the accumulation of money in the market and better redistributing revenue among firms,
 slightly reducing the level of monopoly and,
 consequently,
 the Gini index.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}>1$
\end_inset

,
 workers go to the market more than once before new consumption occurs,
 so there is always less money available with each subsequent visit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
We can consider an extreme situation where a worker withdraws all the money at market value the first time,
 regardless of how many other withdrawal attempts are executed before new consumption occurs,
 no revenue will be possible,
 and we would have the same practical effect as if we had 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 increasing the frequency of application of rule 3 for values above 1 tends to have a less significant effect than that for values below 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
More precisely,
 both changes alter the distribution of GDP among capitalists without altering the total quantity.
 In the first case,
 when we change the fraction,
 we have the same amount of money distributed among capitalists with the same amount of withdrawals from the market.
 But for lower 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{M}$
\end_inset

 values,
 the distribution of GDP among capitalists is more uniform;
 increasing the fraction increases the number of higher and lower value withdrawals from the market value,
 making it more unequal and slightly affecting the overall inequality of the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
In the second case,
 by changing the frequency of withdrawals from the market,
 we alter the average amount of money withdrawn,
 with more significant effects for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}\le1$
\end_inset

,
 as it allows the accumulation of deposits at market value between each withdrawal attempt ,
 since for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{M}>1$
\end_inset

 there is more than one withdrawal attempt before a new deposit is made at market value.
 Since total GDP remains constant,
 fewer visits to the market imply larger withdrawals and a more concentrated profit distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang english
Hiring,
 wage payment and firing rules
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:hiring"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Wright reports that the CSA model presents an unemployment rate of 18.5%,
 a value considered higher than what is normally reported by modern economies.
 This value is originally compared to expanded measures of unemployment,
 as more traditional measures only take into account people who are unemployed and actively looking for work.
 A.G.
 Isaac provides the example that during the Great Recession in the US,
 the U6 unemployment rate reached just over 17%
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ISAAC2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This value accounts for both active job seekers and underemployed or discouraged workers.
 A.G.
 Isaac proposes some modifications aimed at correcting minor implementation errors and making the results more realistic.
 Among the changes introduced,
 the one of particular interest here is the reduction in the pressure for wage increases,
 controlled by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

,
 as defined in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "modifications"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0.01$
\end_inset

.
 In both studies,
 the authors examined only a single fixed value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Lin Lin also draws attention to the problem of high unemployment in Wright's original model and proposes a modified version that achieves a low unemployment rate
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "diss_mods_00007721"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Due to differences in the rules,
 there is not a variable parameter similar to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

,
 but only one scenario is analyzed,
 namely,
 a single level of pressure for wage adjustments by workers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
First,
 we verified that increasing the per capita wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}$
\end_inset

 leads only to a rescaling of the distributions,
 leaving 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 unchanged.
 Therefore,
 to explore variations in 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 we directly modify the wage-bargaining parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 Figure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:reajuste"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 shows how the wealth and income distributions evolve as 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 varies.
 Inequality displays a nonmonotonic dependence on 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

,
 with a minimum around 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\simeq5\%$
\end_inset

 for wealth and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\simeq10\%$
\end_inset

 for income,
 even though the average wage rises steadily with 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 By contrast,
 the labor share increases throughout the explored range of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

,
 while GDP and HHI remain largely insensitive to this parameter(not shown).
\end_layout

