\end_inset

 raises the average wage 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

,
 which in turn reduces the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $R=\overline{w}/\overline{p}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 are inversely related.
 For large enough values of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 (approximately 
\begin_inset Formula $R\gtrsim3$
\end_inset

),
 the model produces realistic unemployment rates (approximately 
\begin_inset Formula $18\%$
\end_inset

,
 from the color scale in the figure),
 displaying increasing inequality as 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 increases.
 This behavior is consistent with results originally obtained under constant wages and is also observed in empirical data.
